{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Shipped in 0.14.0, merged to `main` (`5f631f2`) and pushed. Your real sync now reports: `pushed (20 commit(s) in scope)`.\n\n## The gotcha, laid out cleanly\n\nHere's the thing I explained badly. **A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an append log.**\n\nThe ref points at a commit whose *tree* maps every annotated commit → its note. All of them. So whatever tree you push **becomes the remote's entire notes database**. There's no \"push these few notes\" operation — you always replace the whole thing.\n\nSo the natural implementation — take your local notes ref, filter it down to your branch, push that — does this:\n\n```\nremote before:  { A→note, B→note, C→note }\nyou push:       { C→note }              (your branch only)\nremote after:   { C→note }              ← A and B are gone\n```\n\nAnd because your filtered commit still descends from the remote's tip, **it fast-forwards**. Git accepts it happily. A and B survive in the ref's *history* but vanish from `git notes show` and from any fresh clone. Silent data loss for your collaborators.\n\nThe fix is to build the tree the other way round — start from what the remote has, add yours:\n\n```\nremote before:  { A→note, B→note, C→note }\nyou push:       { A→note, B→note, C→note, D→note }   (remote's tip ∪ your scope)\nremote after:   all four\n```\n\nYour other branches' notes were simply never written into that tree, so they never left your machine. That's it — that's the whole trick, and it's exactly what you said when you landed on \"remote's current content plus subset.\"\n\nOne correction to my earlier framing while I'm here: the *unseeded* naive version (build a fresh ref with only your notes) is actually harmless — git rejects it as non-fast-forward. It's specifically the \"filter my local ref\" version that clobbers, because that one *is* a valid fast-forward. I only found that while writing the test.\n\n## Squash-merge interaction — this decided the implementation\n\nThe scope reuses `resolveAnchors`, the same function reads use, rather than a bare `git rev-list`. That matters more than it sounds: a squash discards the source branch's commits, so its conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them back. A rev-list-only scope would push the mapping events and **strand the events they point at** — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists to save.\n\nI didn't just assert either of these. I simulated both wrong implementations and confirmed the tests fail against them:\n\n- rev-list-only scope → the squash-merge test fails ✓\n- filtered-tree push → the clobber test fails ✓\n\nWorth noting my first version of the clobber test was **vacuous** — I branched from `main` instead of the root, so the sibling's anchor was in scope anyway and the test passed even against the broken build. Caught it only because I ran the simulation.\n\nAlso: **the scan gate now only inspects what the push carries**, so a finding on a branch you aren't pushing no longer blocks unrelated work. That's a direct fix for the class of block you hit tonight.\n\n155 tests, 154 pass, 1 pre-existing skip.\n\n**One limitation I chose not to paper over:** the pre-push hook runs with `</dev/null`, so it can't see which refs git is pushing and scopes by `HEAD`. Push a branch you're not checked out on and it under-sends — safe direction (nothing lost, just not yet shared), and the next sync from that branch ships it. Fixing it properly means changing the installed hook script, which existing installs won't pick up automatically.\n\n## Your findings-printing idea\n\nI roadmapped it rather than building it, because there's a tension worth resolving first: **CI depends on the current behavior.** `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is the documented CI integration, so going quiet by default would break it. The default probably needs to be terminal-aware, or CI opts in with `--report`.\n\nI did capture the part I think is the real insight — that the output should *name the failure mode* rather than just say \"don't look.\" An agent told *why* reading findings reproduces them will comply; a bare prohibition invites a workaround. Also worth noting masking the span (0.6.1) narrowed this loop but didn't close it: `keyword-assignment` trips on the *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is still printed.\n\nAlso still on the list: `readEvents` defaulting to unscoped. That's the read half of the same mismatch you identified, and it belongs in `readEvents` rather than `export` precisely because intent-recall will never touch the CLI.\n\n`scripts/inspect-finding.py` is still untracked, and the design doc still references it.\n\nresult: cledger 0.14.0 on main (5f631f2) — push is now branch-scoped, resolving re_anchor mappings so squash-merged conversations still ship, and building on the remote's tip so a scoped push adds rather than clobbers; both properties pinned by tests proven to fail against the wrong implementations, and the scan gate now only inspects in-scope events.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","dirty_fingerprint":"bbe20560a71a254635cbd033aa353a00fbd2c3fb094525003f36b4423871768b","head":"c841d5d76d898a65f06312edfc538793eb0bdb61","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq":1205},"id":"ev1-4186730c99e1fe2340270f5a345393f13a6d8121cfa1ff5151252738d2a123f7","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-29T04:00:14.282Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.220","tool":"cledger","version":"0.15.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"hig